[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Thread Pools: ExecutorService and Future in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In concurrent programming, managing threads efficiently is crucial for performance. Creating a new thread for every task can be resource-intensive. Thread pools provide a mechanism to reuse threads, reducing the overhead of thread creation and destruction. Java's `ExecutorService` and `Future` interfaces are key components for implementing and managing thread pools."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Thread Pools"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A thread pool is a collection of worker threads that are available to perform tasks. Instead of creating a new thread for each task, tasks are submitted to the thread pool, and a worker thread from the pool picks up the task and executes it. Once the task is complete, the thread returns to the pool and waits for the next task. This approach significantly improves performance, especially for applications that handle a large number of short-lived tasks."},{"order":5,"type":"heading","text":"ExecutorService Interface"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ExecutorService` interface, part of the `java.util.concurrent` package, provides methods for submitting tasks to a thread pool and managing the execution of those tasks. It decouples task submission from task execution. Common implementations include `ThreadPoolExecutor`, `ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor`, `ForkJoinPool`, obtained via static factory methods in the `Executors` class."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an ExecutorService"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Executors` class provides several factory methods for creating different types of thread pools:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`newFixedThreadPool(int nThreads)`: Creates a thread pool with a fixed number of threads."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`newCachedThreadPool()`: Creates a thread pool that creates new threads as needed, but reuses previously created threads when they are available.  Idle threads are terminated after a period of inactivity."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`newSingleThreadExecutor()`: Creates a thread pool with a single thread. Tasks are executed sequentially."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`newScheduledThreadPool(int corePoolSize)`: Creates a thread pool that can schedule tasks to run after a delay or periodically."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class ExecutorServiceExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a fixed thread pool with 3 threads
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        // Submitting tasks to the executor
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            Runnable worker = new WorkerThread("Task " + i);
            executor.execute(worker);
        }

        executor.shutdown(); // Initiates an orderly shutdown
        while (!executor.isTerminated()) {
            // Waiting for all tasks to complete
        }

        System.out.println("All tasks finished");
    }
}

class WorkerThread implements Runnable {
    private String taskName;

    public WorkerThread(String taskName) {
        this.taskName = taskName;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " executing " + taskName);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(2000); // Simulate task execution time
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " finished " + taskName);
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the above example, a fixed thread pool with 3 threads is created. Five tasks are submitted to the executor. The `shutdown()` method initiates an orderly shutdown, meaning that the executor will no longer accept new tasks, but will finish all tasks that have already been submitted. The `isTerminated()` method checks if all tasks have completed."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Submitting Tasks: execute() vs. submit()"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ExecutorService` provides two primary methods for submitting tasks:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`execute(Runnable command)`: Executes the given command at some time in the future. This method is suitable for tasks that do not return a result or throw checked exceptions."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`submit(Runnable task)`: Submits a `Runnable` task for execution and returns a `Future` representing the pending results of the task. The `Future` can be used to check if the task is complete, wait for its completion, and retrieve the result (which will be `null` for `Runnable` tasks)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`submit(Callable<T> task)`: Submits a `Callable` task for execution and returns a `Future` representing the pending result of the task. The `Callable` interface is similar to `Runnable`, but it can return a result and throw checked exceptions."},{"order":20,"type":"heading","text":"Future Interface"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Future` interface represents the result of an asynchronous computation. It provides methods to check if the computation is complete, wait for its completion, and retrieve the result. The `Future` interface is especially useful when you need to retrieve a value from a task or handle exceptions thrown by the task."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Future to Get Results"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FutureExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        // Submitting a Callable task
        Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
            System.out.println("Task started");
            Thread.sleep(3000); // Simulate long running task
            System.out.println("Task finished");
            return 42; // Return the result
        };

        Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

        System.out.println("Doing something else while the task is running...");
        Thread.sleep(1000); // Simulate other work

        // Get the result of the task (blocks until the task is complete)
        try {
            Integer result = future.get();
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);
        } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, a `Callable` task is submitted to the executor. The `submit()` method returns a `Future` object. The `get()` method blocks until the task is complete and returns the result. If the task throws an exception, the `get()` method will throw an `ExecutionException`."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Checking for Task Completion and Cancellation"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Future` interface also provides methods to check if the task is complete and to cancel the task:"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`isDone()`: Returns `true` if the task is complete, either normally, by throwing an exception, or by being cancelled."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`isCancelled()`: Returns `true` if the task was cancelled before it completed normally."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`cancel(boolean mayInterruptIfRunning)`: Attempts to cancel the task. If the task is already completed or cancelled, or if the executor cannot cancel the task, this method will return `false`. The `mayInterruptIfRunning` parameter specifies whether to interrupt the thread executing the task. If `true`, the thread will be interrupted; otherwise, the task will be allowed to complete."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FutureCancellationExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(1);

        Callable<String> task = () -> {
            try {
                System.out.println("Task started");
                Thread.sleep(5000); // Simulate long running task
                System.out.println("Task finished");
                return "Task completed successfully";
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                System.out.println("Task interrupted");
                return "Task was interrupted";
            }
        };

        Future<String> future = executor.submit(task);

        Thread.sleep(1000); // Give the task some time to start

        boolean cancelled = future.cancel(true); // Attempt to cancel the task

        System.out.println("Task cancelled: " + cancelled);
        System.out.println("Task is done: " + future.isDone());
        System.out.println("Task is cancelled: " + future.isCancelled());

        try {
            String result = future.get(); // Attempt to get the result
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);
        } catch (CancellationException e) {
            System.out.println("Task was cancelled and threw an exception.");
        } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the task is cancelled after 1 second. The `cancel(true)` method attempts to interrupt the thread executing the task. The `get()` method throws a `CancellationException` because the task was cancelled."},{"order":32,"type":"heading","text":"Shutting Down the ExecutorService"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's important to properly shut down the `ExecutorService` when it's no longer needed. Failing to do so can lead to resource leaks and prevent the application from exiting. There are two main ways to shut down an `ExecutorService`:"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`shutdown()`: Initiates an orderly shutdown in which previously submitted tasks are executed, but no new tasks will be accepted. This is the preferred method in most cases."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`shutdownNow()`: Attempts to stop all actively executing tasks, halts the processing of waiting tasks, and returns a list of the tasks that were awaiting execution. This method should be used with caution, as it may lead to incomplete or inconsistent results."},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"After calling `shutdown()` or `shutdownNow()`, you can use the `awaitTermination()` method to wait for the executor to terminate. This method blocks until all tasks have completed execution after a shutdown request, or the timeout occurs, or the current thread is interrupted, whichever happens first."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ExecutorShutdownExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            Runnable worker = () -> {
                try {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " executing task.");
                    Thread.sleep(2000); // Simulate task execution
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " finished task.");
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " interrupted.");
                }
            };
            executor.execute(worker);
        }

        executor.shutdown();

        try {
            if (!executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                System.out.println("Executor did not terminate in the given time.");
                executor.shutdownNow(); // Attempt to stop actively executing tasks
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            executor.shutdownNow(); // Preserve interrupt status
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("Executor terminated.");
    }
}"},{"order":38,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"Thread pools, managed through `ExecutorService` and `Future`, are essential for efficient concurrent programming in Java. They allow you to reuse threads, manage task execution, retrieve results, and handle exceptions. Understanding how to create, submit tasks to, and shut down thread pools is crucial for building scalable and responsive applications."}]
